{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "B2",
  "unit": 11,
  "title": "The participle as an adjective",
  "description": "Describe a person by what they have done, or what is being done to them.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "The participle is not only for the passive. It can describe a thing or a person on its own, and then it behaves like an adjective, including the agreement.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Describing with the participle",
        "body": "A participle after the noun describes it: un coche aparcado, a parked car; una mujer cansada, a tired woman. It agrees with the noun like any other adjective: un coche aparcado, but una coche aparcada. And the difference from an adjective is that the participle keeps the action inside it: cansado means tired because of the doing.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "Encontramos un coche aparcado.",
            "en": "We found a parked car."
          },
          {
            "es": "Es una mujer cansada.",
            "en": "She is a tired woman."
          },
          {
            "es": "Tenía las manos heladas.",
            "en": "Her hands were cold."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "How it differs from estar",
        "body": "Estar plus a participle says the action is happening now: está quemando, it is burning. The participle on its own says the state it left behind: la cocina está sucia, the kitchen is dirty, but la cocina está ensuciada, the kitchen has been made dirty, by somebody. Spanish makes that difference, and English mostly does not.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "El motor está recalentado.",
            "en": "The engine is overheated.",
            "note": "The participle goes after the noun."
          },
          {
            "es": "La puerta está cerrada.",
            "en": "The door is closed."
          },
          {
            "es": "El cristal está roto.",
            "en": "The window is broken."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
